title: fail_if_tag_exists never fires - remote_tag_exists returns a hard 0
updated: 2026-08-27T14:40:15Z
---

Client::remote_tag_exists is a stub that returns 0 unconditionally, so the
fail_if_tag_exists check in release() can never trigger. The POD lists it under
CONFIGURATION as working behavior ("Error if tag already exists on remote").

The Recorder fake in t/lib returns 0 as well, so no test can catch the gap.

Either implement the registry query or document the attribute as not yet
implemented. Shipping it as a promise is the one option to avoid.

Documented as not implemented for now, in both the POD and README.md - a user reading either no longer believes the tag is protected. Implementation is NOT done and stays open.

Why it is not a quick fix: the check needs to ask a *registry*, not the local daemon. The engine route for that is GET /distribution/{name}/json, and API::Docker exposes no such resource class (it has images, containers, networks, volumes, system, exec). So this needs the endpoint added in ../p5-api-docker first, then remote_tag_exists wired to it here, then a Recorder case that can return both answers. Filed on that repo's board.

2026-08-27, from the API::Docker side: the endpoint this ticket waits on now
exists. API::Docker 0.004 (unreleased) adds

  $docker->distribution->exists($image_ref, auth => $auth)   # 1 / 0 / croak
  $docker->distribution->inspect($image_ref, auth => $auth)  # descriptor

exists is meant for remote_tag_exists and is callable without an eval. It
croaks rather than answering "no" when the engine has no /distribution route
-- which rootless Podman does not, measured -- so replacing the hard 0 with it
turns "cannot check" into a loud failure instead of a silent false. Decide
whether fail_if_tag_exists should treat that croak as fatal or as a warning.

Auth is optional: no auth argument means an anonymous lookup, which is what a
public image needs. Do not land before API::Docker 0.004 is released.

2026-08-27: implemented, API::Docker 0.004 (installed locally, not yet on CPAN - cpanfile pin raised to 0.004 per rule 9).

Client::remote_tag_exists now asks distribution->exists with the credential from auth_for_image_ref, omitting the auth option entirely when none resolves (anonymous lookup, what a public image needs). The 'cannot ask this engine' croak is deliberately not caught there.

Maintainer's decision on the open question: an engine that cannot answer is FATAL, not a warning. release() distinguishes the two cases - "Tag X already exists on remote registry (ref)" vs "fail_if_tag_exists is set, but this engine cannot answer whether 'ref' ... : <engine text> - set fail_if_tag_exists = 0 to release without the check". Rationale: the default is 0, so whoever turns it on asked for the tag to be protected; downgrading an unanswerable check to a silent 'does not exist' is the bug this ticket describes. Practical consequence, measured: on this machine's rootless Podman every release with fail_if_tag_exists = 1 now stops. Documented in both POD and README, not as a footnote.

Tests: t/52-fail-if-tag-exists.t (exists / croak / not-exists / not consulted when off) and t/56-client-registry-internals.t (auth key omitted rather than passed as undef). Mutation-verified by the orchestrator: degrading the fatal to a log() turns t/52 subtest 2 red, passing auth => undef unconditionally turns t/56 subtest 1 red. lib/ restored and md5-verified afterwards.

POD and README no longer say 'not implemented'.
